the MIT ballroom dance team is an  organization of about 100 members we  have grad students undergrad students as  well as MIT affiliates and their  partners we don't have to be a dancer or  have an experience in order to join the  team we don't have tryouts and we have a  motley of different types of classes to  help you get started  per team itself originated from a  ballroom dance club that was established  in the 1970s and because of the  popularity of ballroom dancing  competitions several Club members  decided to start a ballroom dance team  because they wanted to compete and focus  more on the technical aspects of dancing  rather than the social aspect so we have  a bunch of classes we teach all the way  from beginner all the way to champ and  we have instruction in the four  different styles that comprise ballroom  and it chosen in the way we compete in  the way we perform and we are probably  one of the stronger teams collegiate  teams in the country today  we also host one of the largest  competitions nationally where we have  almost a thousand competitors come in  which is very exciting  focuses on social dancing which means  that we do cover all of the regular ball  advances and we do teach basically all  of them but it's at a pretty relaxed  kind of pace we don't actually require  them to have any partners or any  previous experience it's supposed to be  an environment that's just friendly and  casual a place where people can feel  comfortable coming and going although  the team and the club have slightly  different ways in which we operate in  the end it all comes not the same thing  we all love ballroom dancing and often  you find team members involved in  running some of the things that the club  doesn't and some of the club members our  team members now some people you know  might want to choose to dance one style  competitively but they still want to  learn the other style just to be able to  dance it socially so we are we are part  of one big system there is a pretty  large variety of people's and we do  welcome everybody it ranges from you  know the MIT students and then there are  also some faculty and staff and then  other people who they're just in the  community and they like learning how to  dance  one aspect that really plays into it is  just how passionate people here are at  MIT  you'll see somebody light up about you  know phytoplankton and then at the very  next moment they'll be talking about how  they just love this one Samba song and I  think it's this passion that really  drives people to be willing to commit as  much time as necessary to perfect not  only their academic work but also you  know their dance technique or any other  extracurricular activities that they do  you
 

